    question: "What is the difference between functional and class components?",
    idealAnswer: "**Functional Components** and **Class Components** are two ways to define components in React.\n\n**Functional Components:**\n- JavaScript functions that return JSX\n- Use hooks for state and lifecycle\n- Simpler syntax and less code\n- Better performance (no instance creation)\n\n```jsx\nfunction Welcome({ name }) {\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\n  \n  return (\n    <div>\n      <h1>Hello, {name}!</h1>\n      <p>Count: {count}</p>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n**Class Components:**\n- ES6 classes that extend React.Component\n- Use this.state and this.setState\n- Lifecycle methods (componentDidMount, etc.)\n- More verbose but familiar to OOP developers\n\n```jsx\nclass Welcome extends React.Component {\n  constructor(props) {\n    super(props);\n    this.state = { count: 0 };\n  }\n  \n  render() {\n    return (\n      <div>\n        <h1>Hello, {this.props.name}!</h1>\n        <p>Count: {this.state.count}</p>\n      </div>\n    );\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**Key Differences:**\n| Feature | Functional | Class |\n|---------|------------|-------|\n| Syntax | Function | Class |\n| State | useState hook | this.state |\n| Lifecycle | useEffect hooks | Lifecycle methods |\n| Performance | Better | Slightly slower |\n| Code | Less verbose | More verbose |\n\n**Recommendation**: Use functional components with hooks for new projects."
