    question: "What is one-way data binding in React?",
    idealAnswer: "**One-way data binding** is React's core principle where data flows in a single direction - from parent to child components.\n\n**How It Works:**\n```\nParent Component\n    ↓ (props)\nChild Component\n    ↑ (events/callbacks)\nParent Component\n```\n\n**Example:**\n```jsx\nfunction Parent() {\n  const [message, setMessage] = useState('Hello from Parent');\n  \n  const handleMessageChange = (newMessage) => {\n    setMessage(newMessage);\n  };\n  \n  return (\n    <Child \n      message={message} \n      onMessageChange={handleMessageChange} \n    />\n  );\n}\n\nfunction Child({ message, onMessageChange }) {\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <p>{message}</p>\n      <input \n        value={message}\n        onChange={(e) => onMessageChange(e.target.value)}\n      />\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n**Benefits:**\n- **Predictable**: Data flows in one direction\n- **Debuggable**: Easy to trace data flow\n- **Performant**: Optimizations are easier\n- **Maintainable**: Clear data relationships\n\n**vs Two-Way Binding:**\n- **React**: One-way with callbacks\n- **Angular/Vue**: Two-way with v-model/ngModel\n- **React approach**: More explicit and controllable"
