    question: "What are refs in React?",
    idealAnswer: "**Refs** provide a way to access DOM nodes directly or persist values across renders without causing re-renders.\n\n**Creating Refs:**\n```jsx\nimport { useRef, useEffect } from 'react';\n\nfunction Component() {\n  const inputRef = useRef(null);\n  \n  useEffect(() => {\n    inputRef.current.focus(); // Direct DOM access\n  }, []);\n  \n  return <input ref={inputRef} />;\n}\n```\n\n**Use Cases:**\n\n**1. Managing Focus:**\n```jsx\nfunction SearchInput() {\n  const inputRef = useRef(null);\n  \n  useEffect(() => {\n    inputRef.current.focus();\n  }, []);\n  \n  return <input ref={inputRef} placeholder=\"Search...\" />;\n}\n```\n\n**2. Integrating with Third-Party Libraries:**\n```jsx\nfunction Chart() {\n  const chartRef = useRef(null);\n  \n  useEffect(() => {\n    const chart = new Chart(chartRef.current, config);\n    return () => chart.destroy();\n  }, []);\n  \n  return <canvas ref={chartRef} />;\n}\n```\n\n**3. Storing Mutable Values:**\n```jsx\nfunction Timer() {\n  const intervalRef = useRef(null);\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\n  \n  useEffect(() => {\n    intervalRef.current = setInterval(() => {\n      setCount(c => c + 1);\n    }, 1000);\n    \n    return () => clearInterval(intervalRef.current);\n  }, []);\n  \n  return <div>Count: {count}</div>;\n}\n```\n\n**Forwarding Refs:**\n```jsx\nconst FancyButton = React.forwardRef((props, ref) => (\n  <button ref={ref} className=\"fancy\">\n    {props.children}\n  </button>\n));\n\n// Usage\nconst buttonRef = useRef(null);\n<FancyButton ref={buttonRef}>Click me</FancyButton>\n```\n\n**When NOT to Use Refs:**\n- Avoid for declarative UI updates\n- Don't use for managing form state\n- Prefer state and props for most cases"
