    idealAnswer: "**Lifting State Up** is a pattern where state is moved from child components to their nearest common ancestor to share data between siblings.\n\n**The Problem:**\n```jsx\nfunction Parent() {\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <CounterA /> {/* Independent state */}\n      <CounterB /> {/* Independent state */}\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n// Counters can't share data\n```\n\n**The Solution:**\n```jsx\nfunction Parent() {\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0); // State lifted up\n  \n  return (\n    <div>\n      <CounterA count={count} onIncrement={setCount} />\n      <CounterB count={count} onIncrement={setCount} />\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n\nfunction CounterA({ count, onIncrement }) {\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <p>Counter A: {count}</p>\n      <button onClick={() => onIncrement(count + 1)}>+</button>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n**Real-World Example - Temperature Converter:**\n```jsx\nfunction Calculator() {\n  const [temperature, setTemperature] = useState('');\n  \n  const toCelsius = (f) => (f - 32) * 5 / 9;\n  const toFahrenheit = (c) => (c * 9 / 5) + 32;\n  \n  return (\n    <div>\n      <TemperatureInput \n        scale=\"c\" \n        temperature={temperature}\n        onTemperatureChange={setTemperature}\n      />\n      <TemperatureInput \n        scale=\"f\" \n        temperature={temperature}\n        onTemperatureChange={setTemperature}\n      />\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n**Benefits:**\n- **Single source of truth**: State lives in one place\n- **Data consistency**: All components see same data\n- **Easier debugging**: Centralized state management\n- **Component reusability**: Children become stateless\n\n**When to Use:**\n- Sibling components need to share data\n- Parent needs to know child state\n- Global state needed (consider Context API for complex cases)"
