    question: "What is the Context API and when should you use it?",
    idealAnswer: "**Context API** provides a way to pass data through the component tree without prop drilling.\n\n**Basic Usage:**\n```jsx\n// Create context\nconst ThemeContext = createContext('light');\n\n// Provider component\nfunction App() {\n  const [theme, setTheme] = useState('light');\n  \n  return (\n    <ThemeContext.Provider value={{ theme, setTheme }}>\n      <Toolbar />\n    </ThemeContext.Provider>\n  );\n}\n\n// Consumer component\nfunction Button() {\n  const { theme, setTheme } = useContext(ThemeContext);\n  \n  const style = {\n    background: theme === 'dark' ? '#333' : '#FFF'\n  };\n  \n  return (\n    <button style={style} onClick={() => setTheme(theme === 'dark' ? 'light' : 'dark')}>\n      Toggle Theme\n    </button>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n**When to Use Context:**\n- **Global state**: Theme, language, user authentication\n- **Avoiding prop drilling**: Deep component nesting\n- **Cross-component communication**: Sibling components need shared data\n\n**When NOT to Use Context:**\n- **Local component state**: Keep state in component\n- **High-frequency updates**: Context updates cause all consumers to re-render\n- **Complex state management**: Consider Redux, Zustand, etc.\n\n**Performance Optimization:**\n```jsx\n// Split context to avoid unnecessary re-renders\nconst ThemeContext = createContext();\nconst AuthContext = createContext();\n\n// Memoize context value\nfunction App() {\n  const [theme, setTheme] = useState('light');\n  const themeValue = useMemo(() => ({ theme, setTheme }), [theme]);\n  \n  return (\n    <ThemeContext.Provider value={themeValue}>\n      <Child />\n    </ThemeContext.Provider>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n**Custom Context Hook:**\n```jsx\nfunction useTheme() {\n  const context = useContext(ThemeContext);\n  if (!context) {\n    throw new Error('useTheme must be used within ThemeProvider');\n  }\n  return context;\n}\n```"
