    question: "What are render props?",
    idealAnswer: "**Render Props** is a technique for sharing code between components using a prop whose value is a function.\n\n**Basic Pattern:**\n```jsx\nfunction DataProvider({ render, url }) {\n  const [data, setData] = useState(null);\n  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);\n  \n  useEffect(() => {\n    fetch(url)\n      .then(res => res.json())\n      .then(data => {\n        setData(data);\n        setLoading(false);\n      });\n  }, [url]);\n  \n  return render({ data, loading });\n}\n\n// Usage\n<DataProvider \n  url=\"/api/users\"\n  render={({ data, loading }) => (\n    <div>\n      {loading ? (\n        <div>Loading...</div>\n      ) : (\n        <ul>\n          {data.map(user => (\n            <li key={user.id}>{user.name}</li>\n          ))}\n        </ul>\n      )}\n    </div>\n  )}\n/>\n```\n\n**Using Children as Render Prop:**\n```jsx\nfunction MouseTracker({ children }) {\n  const [position, setPosition] = useState({ x: 0, y: 0 });\n  \n  useEffect(() => {\n    const handleMouseMove = (e) => {\n      setPosition({ x: e.clientX, y: e.clientY });\n    };\n    \n    window.addEventListener('mousemove', handleMouseMove);\n    return () => window.removeEventListener('mousemove', handleMouseMove);\n  }, []);\n  \n  return children(position);\n}\n\n// Usage\n<MouseTracker>\n  {({ x, y }) => (\n    <div>\n      Mouse position: {x}, {y}\n    </div>\n  )}\n</MouseTracker>\n```\n\n**Real-World Example - Data Fetching:**\n```jsx\nfunction ApiProvider({ url, children }) {\n  const [data, setData] = useState(null);\n  const [error, setError] = useState(null);\n  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);\n  \n  useEffect(() => {\n    let cancelled = false;\n    \n    const fetchData = async () => {\n      try {\n        setLoading(true);\n        const response = await fetch(url);\n        const result = await response.json();\n        \n        if (!cancelled) {\n          setData(result);\n          setError(null);\n        }\n      } catch (err) {\n        if (!cancelled) {\n          setError(err.message);\n        }\n      } finally {\n        if (!cancelled) {\n          setLoading(false);\n        }\n      }\n    };\n    \n    fetchData();\n    \n    return () => { cancelled = true; };\n  }, [url]);\n  \n  return children({ data, error, loading });\n}\n\n// Usage\n<ApiProvider url=\"/api/posts\">\n  {({ data, error, loading }) => {\n    if (loading) return <div>Loading...</div>;\n    if (error) return <div>Error: {error}</div>;\n    return <PostList posts={data} />;\n  }}\n</ApiProvider>\n```\n\n**Benefits:**\n- Code reuse and sharing\n- Flexible rendering logic\n- Separation of concerns\n- Composable patterns\n\n**Render Props vs Hooks:**\n- Render props: Component-based pattern\n- Hooks: Function-based pattern\n- Hooks are generally preferred in modern React"
