    question: "What is lazy loading in React?",
    idealAnswer: "**Lazy Loading** is a technique to defer loading of components until they're needed, improving initial load performance.\n\n**React.lazy() and Suspense:**\n```jsx\nimport { lazy, Suspense } from 'react';\n\n// Lazy load component\nconst LazyComponent = lazy(() => import('./LazyComponent'));\n\nfunction App() {\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <h1>My App</h1>\n      <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>\n        <LazyComponent />\n      </Suspense>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n**Route-Based Code Splitting:**\n```jsx\nimport { lazy, Suspense } from 'react';\nimport { BrowserRouter, Routes, Route } from 'react-router-dom';\n\nconst Home = lazy(() => import('./Home'));\nconst About = lazy(() => import('./About'));\nconst Contact = lazy(() => import('./Contact'));\n\nfunction App() {\n  return (\n    <BrowserRouter>\n      <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading page...</div>}>\n        <Routes>\n          <Route path=\"/\" element={<Home />} />\n          <Route path=\"/about\" element={<About />} />\n          <Route path=\"/contact\" element={<Contact />} />\n        </Routes>\n      </Suspense>\n    </BrowserRouter>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n**Component-Based Lazy Loading:**\n```jsx\nfunction App() {\n  const [showComponent, setShowComponent] = useState(false);\n  \n  const LazyComponent = lazy(() => import('./ExpensiveComponent'));\n  \n  return (\n    <div>\n      <button onClick={() => setShowComponent(true)}>\n        Load Component\n      </button>\n      \n      {showComponent && (\n        <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading component...</div>}>\n          <LazyComponent />\n        </Suspense>\n      )}\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n**Dynamic Imports with Error Handling:**\n```jsx\nfunction loadComponent() {\n  return import('./Component')\n    .then(module => module.default)\n    .catch(error => {\n      console.error('Failed to load component:', error);\n      return () => <div>Failed to load component</div>;\n    });\n}\n\nconst LazyComponent = lazy(loadComponent);\n```\n\n**Benefits:**\n- **Faster initial load**: Smaller initial bundle size\n- **Better performance**: Load only what's needed\n- **Reduced bandwidth**: Users download less code\n- **Improved user experience**: Faster perceived load times\n\n**Best Practices:**\n- Use for routes and large components\n- Always wrap with Suspense\n- Provide meaningful fallback UI\n- Consider preloading for critical components\n- Monitor bundle sizes"
