    question: "What is the Context API vs Redux?",
    idealAnswer: "**Context API** and **Redux** are both solutions for state management, but they serve different purposes and have different trade-offs.\n\n**Context API:**\n\n**Pros:**\n- Built into React (no extra dependencies)\n- Simple API (createContext, useContext)\n- Good for low-frequency updates\n- Less boilerplate\n\n**Cons:**\n- Performance issues with high-frequency updates\n- Limited debugging tools\n- No middleware support\n- Can cause unnecessary re-renders\n\n```jsx\n// Context API example\nconst ThemeContext = createContext();\n\nfunction App() {\n  const [theme, setTheme] = useState('light');\n  \n  return (\n    <ThemeContext.Provider value={{ theme, setTheme }}>\n      <Child />\n    </ThemeContext.Provider>\n  );\n}\n\nfunction Child() {\n  const { theme, setTheme } = useContext(ThemeContext);\n  return <button onClick={() => setTheme('dark')}>Toggle</button>;\n}\n```\n\n**Redux:**\n\n**Pros:**\n- Excellent performance with optimizations\n- Powerful debugging tools (Redux DevTools)\n- Middleware support (thunk, saga)\n- Predictable state updates\n- Time-travel debugging\n- Large ecosystem\n\n**Cons:**\n- More boilerplate\n- Learning curve\n- Extra dependency\n- Can be overkill for simple apps\n\n```jsx\n// Redux example\nimport { createStore } from 'redux';\nimport { Provider, useSelector, useDispatch } from 'react-redux';\n\n// Reducer\nfunction themeReducer(state = { theme: 'light' }, action) {\n  switch (action.type) {\n    case 'TOGGLE_THEME':\n      return { theme: state.theme === 'light' ? 'dark' : 'light' };\n    default:\n      return state;\n  }\n}\n\nconst store = createStore(themeReducer);\n\nfunction App() {\n  return (\n    <Provider store={store}>\n      <Child />\n    </Provider>\n  );\n}\n\nfunction Child() {\n  const theme = useSelector(state => state.theme);\n  const dispatch = useDispatch();\n  \n  return (\n    <button onClick={() => dispatch({ type: 'TOGGLE_THEME' })}>\n      Toggle (Current: {theme})\n    </button>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n**When to Use Context:**\n- Theme, language, authentication state\n- Low-frequency updates\n- Simple state sharing\n- Small to medium applications\n\n**When to Use Redux:**\n- Complex state management\n- High-frequency updates\n- Large applications\n- Need for debugging tools\n- Team collaboration\n\n**Modern Alternatives:**\n- **Zustand**: Simpler than Redux, more powerful than Context\n- **Recoil**: Facebook's experimental state management\n- **Jotai**: Atomic state management\n- **Valtio**: Proxy-based state management"
