    question: "What is React Fiber and how does it improve performance?",
    idealAnswer: "**React Fiber** is React's new reconciliation algorithm introduced in React 16, enabling incremental rendering and better performance.\n\n**What Fiber Solves:**\n- **Blocking rendering**: Long tasks block the main thread\n- **No interruption**: Cannot pause rendering work\n- **Poor priority handling**: All updates have same priority\n- **Limited control**: Cannot schedule work efficiently\n\n**Fiber Architecture:**\n```jsx\n// Each Fiber node contains:\nconst fiberNode = {\n  type: 'div',              // Component type\n  key: null,               // React key\n  elementType: 'div',      // Effective element type\n  stateNode: domElement,   // Actual DOM node\n  \n  // Fiber tree structure\n  return: null,            // Parent fiber\n  child: null,             // First child fiber\n  sibling: null,           // Next sibling fiber\n  index: 0,                // Index in parent\n  \n  // Effects and scheduling\n  effectTag: 0,            // Side effect flags\n  nextEffect: null,        // Next effect in list\n  expirationTime: 0,       // When to render\n  \n  // Memoization\n  memoizedState: null,     // Component state\n  memoizedProps: null,     // Component props\n  \n  // Updates\n  updateQueue: null,       // State updates\n  alternate: null          // Pointer to work-in-progress tree\n};\n```\n\n**Key Features:**\n\n**1. Incremental Rendering:**\n```jsx\n// Old Stack Reconciler (blocking)\nfunction render() {\n  updateComponent(App);      // Blocks until complete\n  commitChanges();           // Apply all changes\n}\n\n// New Fiber Reconciler (interruptible)\nfunction render() {\n  while (hasWork && shouldContinue) {\n    performUnitOfWork();     // Work in small chunks\n  }\n  \n  if (highPriorityWork) {\n    handleHighPriorityWork(); // Interrupt for priority\n  }\n  \n  scheduleMoreWork();        // Resume when time permits\n}\n```\n\n**2. Priority Scheduling:**\n```jsx\n// Different update priorities\nconst UpdatePriority = {\n  Synchronous: 1,    // Sync updates (input, click)\n  Animation: 2,      // Animation updates\n  Transition: 3,     // Route transitions\n  Idle: 4,          // Background updates\n  Normal: 5         // Regular updates\n};\n```\n\n**3. Time Slicing:**\n```jsx\n// Fiber works in time slices\nfunction workLoopConcurrent() {\n  while (workInProgress !== null && !shouldYield()) {\n    performUnitOfWork(workInProgress);\n  }\n}\n\nfunction shouldYield() {\n  // Check if we've exceeded time slice\n  return getCurrentTime() >= deadline;\n}\n```\n\n**Render Phases:**\n```jsx\n// 1. Render Phase (interruptible)\nfunction render() {\n  // Create work-in-progress tree\n  const rootFiber = createWorkInProgress(current, pendingProps);\n  \n  // Process fibers in priority order\n  while (rootFiber) {\n    workLoop(rootFiber);\n  }\n  \n  return rootFiber;\n}\n\n// 2. Commit Phase (non-interruptible)\nfunction commit(rootFiber) {\n  // Apply all changes to DOM\n  commitBeforeMutationEffects(rootFiber);\n  commitMutationEffects(rootFiber);\n  commitLayoutEffects(rootFiber);\n}\n```\n\n**Performance Benefits:**\n- **Non-blocking**: UI remains responsive during updates\n- **Priority-based**: Important updates happen first\n- **Better UX**: Smoother animations and interactions\n- **Concurrent features**: Enables Suspense and concurrent mode\n\n**Real-World Impact:**\n- Large lists render without blocking\n- Smooth animations during state updates\n- Better performance on low-end devices\n- Foundation for future React features"
