    idealAnswer: "**React Server Components (RSC)** are components that run exclusively on the server, enabling direct access to server-side resources and improving performance.\n\n**Key Concepts:**\n\n**1. Server-Only Components**\n```jsx\n// Server Component (runs only on server)\nasync function ServerComponent() {\n  const data = await fetch('https://api.example.com/data');\n  const posts = await data.json();\n  \n  return (\n    <div>\n      <h1>Latest Posts</h1>\n      {posts.map(post => (\n        <article key={post.id}>\n          <h2>{post.title}</h2>\n          <p>{post.content}</p>\n        </article>\n      ))}\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n**2. Client Components**\n```jsx\n// Client Component (runs in browser)\n'use client';\n\nfunction InteractiveButton() {\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\n  \n  return (\n    <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>\n      Clicked {count} times\n    </button>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n**Benefits:**\n- **Zero JavaScript**: Server components don't ship to client\n- **Direct data access**: Can access databases, APIs directly\n- **Better caching**: Server-side caching improves performance\n- **Smaller bundles**: Less client-side JavaScript\n\n**Limitations:**\n- No interactivity (no useState, useEffect)\n- No browser APIs (window, document)\n- Cannot use event handlers"
