    question: "What is React Suspense and how does it work?",
    idealAnswer: "**React Suspense** is a mechanism for components to suspend rendering while they're waiting for something, typically data fetching or code splitting.\n\n**Basic Usage:**\n```jsx\nimport { Suspense } from 'react';\n\nfunction App() {\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <h1>My App</h1>\n      <Suspense fallback={<Loading />}> \n        <LazyComponent />\n      </Suspense>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n**1. Code Splitting**\n```jsx\nimport { lazy, Suspense } from 'react';\n\nconst LazyComponent = lazy(() => import('./LazyComponent'));\n\nfunction App() {\n  return (\n    <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>\n      <LazyComponent />\n    </Suspense>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n**2. Data Fetching with Suspense**\n- Components can throw promises to suspend rendering\n- Suspense catches the promise and shows fallback\n- When promise resolves, component re-renders\n\n**3. Nested Suspense**\n- Multiple suspense boundaries can be nested\n- Each boundary handles its own loading state\n- Provides granular loading experiences\n\n**Benefits:**\n- **Declarative loading**: Show fallback UI naturally\n- **Code splitting**: Load components on demand\n- **Better UX**: Smooth loading experiences"
